			},
			"6": {
				"attributes": {
					"alignment": {
						"type": "integer",
						"value": 0
					},
					"fill": {
						"type": "bool",
						"value": false
					},
					"font-size": {
						"type": "float",
						"value": 19
					},
					"text": {
						"type": "string",
						"value": "TRIGGER NODES\n\nLet's begin at the beginning: generating triggers.\n\nWe've got the Trigger In node and Trigger node.\nThey are both identical with the only difference being that the Trigger In node is interactive and will show up in the Dashboard panel and therefore the Arena & Avenue interface.\n\nThere is not much technical to say about these nodes.\nPress the button, and it sends out a trigger.\nBut do note that these triggers can be animated in Arena\/Avenue via timelines and envelopes.\n\nThe Metronome node will supply you with a constant stream of triggers.\nThe interval between triggers is set by the frequency of the Metronome.\n\nAssignment: toggle the \"enable\"-parameter on the Metronome node.\n\nAs you might have noticed, you can connect multiple sources of triggers to a single inlet.\nThis is one of the unique advantages that the event flow brings to the table.\nThis enables you to create both automatic triggers (via Metronome or Transport Beat) and manual triggers (via Trigger In) in the same patch."
					}
				},
				"bounds": {
					"height": 823,
					"width": 944.6397705078125,
					"x": 320.25,
					"y": 191.5
				},
				"class": {
					"id": "77697265-3C1D-467D-A722-0FC566538374",
					"version": 1
				},
				"clock": "video",
				"color": "ffff6a00",
				"constants": {},
				"hidden": [
					"text",
					"alignment",
					"font-size",
					"fill"
				],
				"name": "Group",
				"thumbnail_visible": true
			},
			"7": {
				"attributes": {
					"alignment": {
						"type": "integer",
						"value": 0
					},
					"fill": {
						"type": "bool",
						"value": true
					},
					"font-size": {
						"type": "float",
						"value": 19
					},
					"text": {
						"type": "string",
						"value": "TRIGGERS\n\nYou've already had a little taste of triggers in the Randomness tutorial.\nIn this tutorial we're going full Nancy-Sinatra-You-Shot-Me-Down-Bang-Bang and teach you about the trigger type.\n\nTriggers are a unique type that only work at event flow.\nThey are also unique in that they cannot be implicitly converted to numerical values.\n\nTiggers are used to trigger one-shot events.\nFor example: start playback of a video, rewind a timeline, generate a random value, reset an oscillator, increase a counter or activate an envelope.\n\nTriggers are also generated by the Transport Beat node which will open the doors to BPM-synced visuals (we've got a dedicated tutorial for this, no worries!)"
					}
				},
